PHONE

TITLE
Real-time Embedded Control Systems and Ptolemy II

CHAPTER
02.cad

ABSTRACT 

Most of the domains that have been explored in conjunction with Ptolemy II
have been <i>data driven</i>.  In a data driven domain, there is a certain
amount of data in the system and every piece of data must be operated 
on, regardless of how long it takes.  Such a domain usually has some 
very nice properties.  Most importantly, they are <i>data deterministic</i>,
meaning that their outputs are a strict function of their inputs.
These domains are very useful for many applications, such as simulating 
digital circuits, or the execution of a signal processing system.
<p>
Unfortunately in the case of real-time systems, especially systems
involved with controlling physical objects, these domains are very
inadequate.  In real-time control systems, data determinism is much 
less important than <i>time determinism</i>.  In other words, the 
control system must analyze its inputs and determine the future state 
of the physical system within some amount of time.
<p>
In particular, this work is oriented around implementing the port-based objects
model of computation as a domain within Ptolemy II.  The port-based object(PBO)
domain has been shown to be an appropriate abstraction for designing 
controllers for real-time systems.  Ptolemy II can be used to combine a
PBO model of the system controller with a model of the system itself for 
system simulation.  Other Ptolemy II domains could also be used to model 
hardware components of an embedded system, enabling hardware-software 
co-simulation. Furthermore, by using a microcontroller running an 
Embedded Java Virtual Machine, the model of the system controller 
in Ptolemy II can translate directly into an java-based controller 
implementation.

